<section aria-labelledby="unit-overview">
  <h2 id="unit-overview">Chapter 1 overview</h2>
  <p>
    Martin begins the book by showing why electronic-structure theory became the central language for describing many-body materials phenomena. The same electronic wavefunction and one-electron spectrum concepts that explain molecules also control the collective behavior of solids, surfaces, and interfaces.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chapter traces the practical problem as an historical sequence: broad insight from first principles is useful only when it can be turned into a controllable approximation strategy.
  </p>
</section>

