<section aria-labelledby="section-01-independent-electron">
  <h2 id="section-01-independent-electron">Why independent-electron pictures work so widely</h2>
  <p>
    Independent-particle reasoning is introduced as a working bridge: replace full many-body complexity with effective states that reproduce key observables. This perspective is highly useful for bonding, transport tendencies, and many ground-state trends.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chapter also flags the boundary: that bridge is not exact. Correlation effects, screened interactions, and excited-state observables remain sensitive to the approximation level.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-01-correlation-pressure">
  <h2 id="section-01-correlation-pressure">Where many-body physics forces methodological change</h2>
  <p>
    Chapter 1 introduces correlation pressure as the main reason one-electron narratives cannot be the end of the story. It motivates variational ideas and density-based formulations as alternative routes that keep calculations tractable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rather than presenting every historical variant, the chapter uses these pressures to set up later choices: where Kohn-Sham models are reliable, where they are useful but limited, and where post-DFT corrections are typically required.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-01-foundational-impact">
  <h2 id="section-01-foundational-impact">Why the field became foundational for materials discovery</h2>
  <p>
    The chapter closes by arguing that electronic-structure methods are no longer auxiliary calculations. They are now tightly coupled to synthesis routes, pressure-driven phase exploration, defect design, and electronic functional prediction.
  </p>
  <p>
    This argument justifies the modern emphasis on reproducibility: observables of interest are method-, basis-, and convergence-sensitive, so the scientific pipeline must be explicit about its model boundary conditions.
  </p>
</section>
